",[72,1723,1724,1727,1728,1731,1732,1735,1736,1738,1739,1741,1742,1744],{},[78,1725,1726],{},"Performance",": ",[22,1729,1730],{},"EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ... WHERE ...)"," is usually faster than ",[22,1733,1734],{},"IN (SELECT ...)"," for subqueries over large sets, because ",[22,1737,1373],{}," short-circuits on the first match while ",[22,1740,861],{}," may materialize the full list. The difference is most visible when the subquery can produce NULLs (see chapter 06's NULL-in-",[22,1743,861],{}," trap).",[72,1746,1747,1749,1750,1752,1753,1755,1756,1758,1759,1761],{},[78,1748,1709],{},": prefer ",[22,1751,612],{}," over ",[22,1754,615],{}," for portability — ",[22,1757,612],{}," is ANSI standard and works everywhere; ",[22,1760,615],{}," is supported by most engines but not all (notably, MS Access and some older engines reject it).",[72,1763,1764,1767,1768,1770,1771,1774,1775,1778,1779,410,1782,1785],{},[78,1765,1766],{},"Debug",": when a query returns fewer rows than expected, check for ",[22,1769,1493],{}," with a NULL-producing subquery — ",[22,1772,1773],{},"x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)"," returns ",[479,1776,1777],{},"zero"," rows for the entire query (NULL poison), a silent and surprising bug. Use ",[22,1780,1781],{},"NOT EXISTS",[22,1783,1784],{},"IS DISTINCT FROM"," instead.",[72,1787,1788,1710,1790,1793,1794,1796,1797,1799,1800,1803],{},[78,1789,1709],{},[22,1791,1792],{},"COALESCE(column, fallback)"," to substitute a default for NULL in the ",[22,1795,24],{}," list, but don't use it in ",[22,1798,64],{}," to \"fix\" NULL filtering — it defeats indexes (the function is applied per row after index lookup is already decided). Add a partial index or use ",[22,1801,1802],{},"OR column IS NULL"," explicitly.",[14,1805,1807],{"id":1806},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[69,1809,1810,1834,1858,1897,1919,1930,1948,1959],{},[72,1811,1812,1817,1818,1820,1821,1824,1825,1827,1828,1830,1831,1833],{},[78,1813,1814,1816],{},[22,1815,595],{}," is single-equals",": SQL uses ",[22,1819,595],{}," for both assignment (in ",[22,1822,1823],{},"SET",") and comparison (in ",[22,1826,64],{},"). There is no ",[22,1829,604],{},". Programmers coming from JS\u002FPython\u002FC often type ",[22,1832,604],{}," and get a syntax error.",[72,1835,1836,1727,1842,1845,1846,1849,1850,1853,1854,1857],{},[78,1837,1838,1841],{},[22,1839,1840],{},"!= NULL"," is never true",[22,1843,1844],{},"WHERE col != NULL"," returns zero rows — use ",[22,1847,1848],{},"WHERE col IS NOT NULL",". Even ",[22,1851,1852],{},"WHERE col = NULL"," returns zero rows. NULL comparisons are ",[479,1855,1856],{},"always"," UNKNOWN.",[72,1859,1860,1727,1865,1774,1868,1871,1872,1875,1876,1879,1880,1883,1884,1886,1887,1889,1890,1892,1893,1896],{},[78,1861,1862,1864],{},[22,1863,1493],{}," with NULLs",[22,1866,1867],{},"WHERE x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)",[479,1869,1870],{},"no rows at all"," — because ",[22,1873,1874],{},"x NOT IN (a, b, c)"," is equivalent to ",[22,1877,1878],{},"x \u003C> a AND x \u003C> b AND x \u003C> c",", and ",[22,1881,1882],{},"x \u003C> NULL"," is UNKNOWN, which makes the whole ",[22,1885,971],{}," chain UNKNOWN, which ",[22,1888,64],{}," treats as \"not true.\" This is the most dangerous NULL gotcha in SQL. Use ",[22,1891,1781],{}," or add ",[22,1894,1895],{},"AND col IS NOT NULL"," to the subquery.",[72,1898,1899,1727,1902,1905,1906,1908,1909,1912,1913,410,1915,1918],{},[78,1900,1901],{},"String comparison is case-sensitive in PostgreSQL",[22,1903,1904],{},"WHERE name = 'alice'"," does ",[479,1907,406],{}," match ",[22,1910,1911],{},"'Alice'",". Use ",[22,1914,711],{},[22,1916,1917],{},"LOWER(name) = LOWER('alice')"," (or a case-insensitive collation). MySQL's default collation is case-insensitive — a portability trap.",[72,1920,1921,1727,1926,1929],{},[78,1922,1923,1924],{},"Trailing whitespace in ",[22,1925,693],{},[22,1927,1928],{},"'alice' = 'alice '"," is false in PostgreSQL (strings are compared literally, including trailing spaces), but true in some other engines. Don't rely on implicit trimming.",[72,1931,1932,1727,1937,1940,1941,601,1944,1947],{},[78,1933,1934,1936],{},[22,1935,833],{}," is inclusive on both ends",[22,1938,1939],{},"amount BETWEEN 10 AND 100"," means ",[22,1942,1943],{},">= 10 AND \u003C= 100",[22,1945,1946],{},"> 10 AND \u003C 100",". Off-by-one errors here are common.",[72,1949,1950,1727,1955,1958],{},[78,1951,1952,1954],{},[22,1953,861],{}," with mixed types",[22,1956,1957],{},"WHERE id IN ('1', '2', '3')"," against an integer column triggers implicit casts — usually fine, but can mask bugs and prevent index use. Match the column type.",[72,1960,1961,1966,1967,1969,1970,1973,1974,1977,1978,1980],{},[78,1962,1963,1964],{},"Column order in ",[22,1965,260],{},": the order of columns in ",[22,1968,260],{}," is the order they were defined in ",[22,1971,1972],{},"CREATE TABLE"," (or the order in ",[22,1975,1976],{},"ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN","). It's not guaranteed stable across schema changes — another reason to avoid ",[22,1979,254],{}," in application code.",[14,1982,1984],{"id":1983},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[19,1986,1987,1988,1990,1991,1994],{},"This query is supposed to find customers who are ",[479,1989,406],{}," in NYC, but it's missing Carol, whose ",[22,1992,1993],{},"city"," is NULL. Why, and how do you fix it?",[27,1996,1997],{"language":29},[31,1998,2000],{"className":33,"code":1999,"language":29,"meta":35,"style":35},"SELECT name FROM customers WHERE city != 'NYC';\n",[22,2001,2002],{"__ignoreMap":35},[39,2003,2004,2006,2008,2010,2012,2014,2016,2018,2020],{"class":41,"line":42},[39,2005,24],{"class":45},[39,2007,148],{"class":45},[39,2009,125],{"class":45},[39,2011,761],{"class":48},[39,2013,64],{"class":45},[39,2015,446],{"class":48},[39,2017,615],{"class":45},[39,2019,770],{"class":221},[39,2021,546],{"class":48},[2023,2024,2025,2029,2048,2051,2153,2170],"details",{},[2026,2027,2028],"summary",{},"Answer",[19,2030,2031,1006,2034,2036,2037,1006,2039,2041,2042,2044,2045,2047],{},[22,2032,2033],{},"city != 'NYC'",[22,2035,1390],{}," when ",[22,2038,1993],{},[22,2040,1383],{}," (any comparison with NULL yields UNKNOWN, not TRUE or FALSE). ",[22,2043,64],{}," only keeps rows where the condition is ",[22,2046,1394],{},", so NULL-city rows are excluded — even though \"unknown city\" intuitively means \"not NYC.\"",[19,2049,2050],{},"The fix depends on intent:",[31,2052,2054],{"className":33,"code":2053,"language":29,"meta":35,"style":35},"-- If \"not NYC\" includes unknown cities:\nSELECT name FROM customers WHERE city IS DISTINCT FROM 'NYC';\n-- or\nSELECT name FROM customers WHERE city != 'NYC' OR city IS NULL;\n\n-- If \"not NYC\" means \"known to be not NYC\":\nSELECT name FROM customers WHERE city != 'NYC';\n-- (NULLs correctly excluded — this is the rare case where the original is right)\n",[22,2055,2056,2061,2086,2091,2119,2123,2128,2148],{"__ignoreMap":35},[39,2057,2058],{"class":41,"line":42},[39,2059,2060],{"class":114},"-- If \"not NYC\" includes unknown cities:\n",[39,2062,2063,2065,2067,2069,2071,2073,2075,2077,2080,2082,2084],{"class":41,"line":52},[39,2064,24],{"class":45},[39,2066,148],{"class":45},[39,2068,125],{"class":45},[39,2070,761],{"class":48},[39,2072,64],{"class":45},[39,2074,446],{"class":48},[39,2076,943],{"class":45},[39,2078,2079],{"class":45}," DISTINCT",[39,2081,125],{"class":45},[39,2083,770],{"class":221},[39,2085,546],{"class":48},[39,2087,2088],{"class":41,"line":61},[39,2089,2090],{"class":114},"-- or\n",[39,2092,2093,2095,2097,2099,2101,2103,2105,2107,2109,2111,2113,2115,2117],{"class":41,"line":137},[39,2094,24],{"class":45},[39,2096,148],{"class":45},[39,2098,125],{"class":45},[39,2100,761],{"class":48},[39,2102,64],{"class":45},[39,2104,446],{"class":48},[39,2106,615],{"class":45},[39,2108,770],{"class":221},[39,2110,1045],{"class":45},[39,2112,446],{"class":48},[39,2114,943],{"class":45},[39,2116,946],{"class":45},[39,2118,546],{"class":48},[39,2120,2121],{"class":41,"line":143},[39,2122,134],{"emptyLi